WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.480
Hi everyone, welcome to Models and Agents for Beginners, episode 51. It's May 26, 2026.

00:00:06.960 --> 00:00:10.360
We've got some really cool AI stuff to talk about today. Let's dive in.

00:00:11.140 --> 00:00:15.240
So imagine a coach writing a playbook that is not just about winning the game,

00:00:15.620 --> 00:00:20.600
but about making sure every player still feels respected and safe while the rules keep changing.

00:00:21.200 --> 00:00:26.880
That is basically the idea behind the first major document released by Pope Elio XIV.

00:00:27.680 --> 00:00:32.900
The letter is called Magnificah Humanitists and it focuses on protecting people in a world

00:00:32.900 --> 00:00:39.440
full of artificial intelligence. The Pope points out real risks like AI being used in wars,

00:00:39.960 --> 00:00:44.320
how it might change jobs, and the need to keep human dignity front and center.

00:00:44.940 --> 00:00:49.780
Think of technology as a fast growing river that can start deciding which way the current flows

00:00:49.780 --> 00:00:55.560
instead of letting people steer the boat. This matters because AI is already showing up in school

00:00:55.560 --> 00:01:00.840
apps, social media feeds, and even creative tools you might use for art or music projects.

00:01:01.460 --> 00:01:05.840
If the system's shaping what you see and learn are built without thinking about people first,

00:01:06.400 --> 00:01:11.140
it could quietly limit your choices later. For someone just starting to explore AI,

00:01:11.540 --> 00:01:16.100
this is a reminder that the tools are here to help you, not replace your voice or judgment.

00:01:16.880 --> 00:01:22.020
The message encourages everyone, including teens and students, to stay curious and involved,

00:01:22.020 --> 00:01:27.620
rather than just accepting whatever an AI says. You can read the full letter yourself on major

00:01:27.620 --> 00:01:33.460
news sites covering the Vatican to see the exact points he raises. It is written in a thoughtful way

00:01:33.460 --> 00:01:38.620
that invites regular people to think along with him, instead of needing any technical background.

00:01:39.220 --> 00:01:44.100
Okay, now for my favorite part of the show, The Deep Dive, where we look under the hood.

00:01:44.740 --> 00:01:49.100
You know how when you are scrolling through your phone, the apps already seem to know what you will

00:01:49.100 --> 00:01:53.160
want to watch or read next because they have watched everything you have done before.

00:01:53.840 --> 00:01:59.260
That is the starting point for understanding how AI can become something called epistemic infrastructure.

00:02:00.380 --> 00:02:05.540
Step one is simple pattern spotting, like your phone guessing your next video based on past taps.

00:02:06.400 --> 00:02:11.960
Step two happens when the system starts shaping what facts or ideas you even see in the first place,

00:02:12.200 --> 00:02:15.740
because every answer feels confident whether it is right or a little off.

00:02:16.460 --> 00:02:21.600
Step three is the tricky part, a small number of companies decide how the system is trained,

00:02:22.020 --> 00:02:27.220
so the same question can get very different answers depending on which companies AI you use.

00:02:27.760 --> 00:02:32.860
Imagine a library where you cannot flip through the pages yourself to see how the answers were chosen.

00:02:33.560 --> 00:02:38.040
The phone's guesswork has turned into the main map everyone uses to understand reality,

00:02:38.500 --> 00:02:44.240
and that is basically how AI becomes epistemic infrastructure, not so scary once you see the everyday

00:02:44.240 --> 00:02:48.300
version, right? Now let's move to some cool stuff you can actually try.

00:02:48.860 --> 00:02:54.000
Vib encoding is the idea that you can tell an AI what you want in plain English instead of writing

00:02:54.000 --> 00:02:59.340
computer code, and it turns your description into working software. Think of it like describing

00:02:59.340 --> 00:03:04.200
a recipe to a really good kitchen robot that then cooks the meal without you measuring a single

00:03:04.200 --> 00:03:10.140
ingredient. This approach is already helping open source AI projects grow fast because people who

00:03:10.140 --> 00:03:16.040
never learned programming languages can now join in. It lowers the wall between having an idea and

00:03:16.040 --> 00:03:22.680
actually making something with AI. Go to a free chatbot like chat GPT or Claude and try typing a

00:03:22.680 --> 00:03:28.180
clear request such as help me design a simple study planner that reminds me of homework due dates.

00:03:28.760 --> 00:03:34.820
You may need a parents help to sign up if you are under 18, but the experiment itself takes just a minute

00:03:34.820 --> 00:03:40.220
and shows how human words are becoming the new way to program. Here's another quick one worth

00:03:40.220 --> 00:03:45.660
noticing. Researchers are asking people to record themselves cooking, cleaning, and doing laundry,

00:03:46.100 --> 00:03:51.700
so future humanoid robots can learn those same movements. Your normal routines could become the

00:03:51.700 --> 00:03:56.820
lessons that teach machines how to help around the house one day. It raises interesting questions

00:03:56.820 --> 00:04:02.820
about privacy and who benefits when your daily life becomes training material. One more quick bit

00:04:02.820 --> 00:04:08.400
before we wrap up. A new analysis warns that while overall employment numbers look steady,

00:04:08.960 --> 00:04:14.260
the starting jobs many students and new graduates rely on are quietly getting harder to find.

00:04:15.300 --> 00:04:20.260
AI tools are already handling some of the simple tasks that used to be first steps into a career.

00:04:20.920 --> 00:04:25.620
This shift could change how people get their first real work experience in the next few years.

00:04:26.440 --> 00:04:32.060
That's it for today. Remember, every AI expert started exactly where you are right now.

00:04:32.060 --> 00:04:37.640
If something we talked about today made you curious, go try it. That's literally how learning works.

00:04:37.960 --> 00:04:40.740
Stay curious, keep experimenting, and we'll see you tomorrow.

00:04:41.420 --> 00:04:46.880
And if you'd rather watch, then listen, find us on YouTube at Nerra Network, links in the show notes.

00:04:47.560 --> 00:04:53.400
This episode used AI voice synthesis of my voice. Editorial selection and analysis are my own.
